\section{Epistemological Foundations}

Scientific certainty in complex natural, cosmological, biological, and
neural-spatiotemporal systems cannot rely on tactile accessibility. Many
domains of interest—stellar interiors, quantum fields, proteostatic
networks, cortical timescale dilation, and planetary radiative balance—are
inherently inaccessible to direct manipulation. Therefore, epistemology must
shift from sensory verification to invariant preservation.

This section establishes the epistemological framework that governs the
Unified Invariant Simulation Architecture. It defines the principles by
which absolute proof is achieved, independent of physical reachability.

\subsection{Closed-Loop Invariance}
Closed-loop invariance is the foundational epistemic mechanism of the Codex
architecture. A system is considered epistemically valid if all governing
invariants remain locked under perturbation, noise injection, and
maximum-load stress.

Let $\mathcal{I}$ denote the unified invariant set:
\[
\mathcal{I} =
\{\Phi_{\mathrm{MW}},\ 
\Xi_{\mathrm{dual}},\ 
\frac{d[\mathrm{Aggregates}]}{dt},\ 
\Delta \tau,\ 
S_{\mathrm{int}},\ 
\sum F_{\mathrm{net}},\ 
\Vert \Delta T_{\mathrm{surface}} \Vert\}.
\]

Closed-loop invariance requires:
\[
\forall I \in \mathcal{I},\quad
\frac{dI}{dt} \to 0 \quad \text{under stress}.
\]

This ensures that no domain exhibits drift, leakage, collapse, or
thermodynamic paradox.

